CAT = "cscsLegal"; SEG = "LEG"
Q = [
("Which Act sets out the general duties of employers and workers for health and safety in Great Britain?",
 "Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974", ["Factories and Workshops Act 1961", "Construction Safety Act 2015", "Building Safety Act 2022"],
 "The Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974 is the main law. Regulations such as CDM 2015 are made under it and add detail for construction."),
("Under health and safety law, what must you do as a worker on site?",
 "Take reasonable care of yourself and others", ["Leave all safety matters to the employer", "Only follow rules your trade sets", "Report hazards only if you are a supervisor"],
 "The 1974 Act requires employees to take reasonable care of their own health and safety and that of others, and to co-operate with their employer. Safety is not left to management alone."),
("What is the main purpose of a risk assessment?",
 "To find hazards and decide controls", ["To record who caused an accident", "To work out the cost of the job", "To list the tools each trade will need"],
 "A risk assessment identifies what could cause harm, who could be harmed and how, and what controls are needed to reduce the risk. It is done before the work starts."),
("What does a method statement describe?",
 "How the task will be done safely", ["Who will be paid for each stage of work", "Which materials the client has chosen", "How long each worker spends on site"],
 "A method statement sets out the safe sequence of work and the controls from the risk assessment. You should be briefed on it before starting the task."),
("When should you normally receive a site induction?",
 "Before you start work on that site", ["After your first week on site", "Only if you are new to construction", "When the site manager has time"],
 "Every worker should have a site induction before starting, covering site rules, hazards, welfare and emergency arrangements. Experience elsewhere does not replace it."),
("What is a toolbox talk?",
 "A short briefing on a safety topic", ["A check of your tools by a manager", "A training course leading to a card", "A meeting to agree pay rates"],
 "Toolbox talks are short, informal sessions, often at the workplace, on a specific hazard or task. They keep the team up to date on risks and changes."),
("You are asked to use a machine you have not been trained on. What should you do?",
 "Refuse and ask for training first", ["Read the manual and try it slowly", "Ask a workmate to show you quickly", "Use it only while no one is nearby"],
 "You must not use equipment you are not trained and competent to use. Tell your supervisor and wait until you have had proper training."),
("Under CDM 2015, who must plan, manage and monitor the construction phase where there is more than one contractor?",
 "The principal contractor", ["The principal designer", "The client's architect", "The local building control officer"],
 "CDM 2015 gives the principal contractor the duty to plan, manage and co-ordinate the construction phase, including the construction phase plan and site inductions. The principal designer covers the pre-construction phase."),
("A construction project must be notified to HSE when it will last more than 30 working days with more than 20 workers at once, or when it will exceed what?",
 "500 person days", ["100 person days", "250 person days", "1,000 person days"],
 "Under CDM 2015 a project is notifiable if it lasts more than 30 working days with more than 20 workers working at the same time, or exceeds 500 person days. The client sends the notice (form F10)."),
("What can an HSE inspector issue to stop a dangerous activity straight away?",
 "A prohibition notice", ["An improvement notice", "A warning letter", "A fixed penalty notice"],
 "A prohibition notice stops an activity that involves a risk of serious personal injury, often with immediate effect. An improvement notice gives time to put something right."),
("What does an improvement notice from an HSE inspector require?",
 "A breach to be put right in a set time", ["Work on site to stop immediately", "The company to pay an on-the-spot fine", "The site manager to be replaced at once"],
 "An improvement notice says what law is being broken and gives a period, at least 21 days, to put it right. It does not stop work by itself."),
("Which regulations require serious workplace injuries and dangerous occurrences to be reported to the enforcing authority?",
 "RIDDOR", ["COSHH", "CDM 2015", "LOLER"],
 "The Reporting of Injuries, Diseases and Dangerous Occurrences Regulations 2013 (RIDDOR) cover reportable injuries, occupational diseases and dangerous occurrences. COSHH covers hazardous substances."),
("Under RIDDOR, an injury must be reported if it keeps a worker off normal duties for more than how many days?",
 "7 days", ["3 days", "5 days", "14 days"],
 "Injuries that incapacitate a worker for more than 7 consecutive days, not counting the day of the accident, must be reported within 15 days. Over 3 day injuries must be recorded but not reported."),
("What is a near miss?",
 "An event that nearly caused harm", ["An accident causing a minor injury", "A hazard that has been removed", "A deadline that is nearly missed"],
 "A near miss is an unplanned event that did not cause injury or damage but could have. Reporting near misses lets the cause be fixed before someone is hurt."),
("Why should near misses be reported?",
 "So the cause can be fixed early", ["So the person involved can be warned", "So the site can claim insurance", "So HSE can fine the contractor"],
 "Near misses show where the next accident could happen. Reporting them lets the site change the method or controls before anyone is injured."),
("What is a permit to work?",
 "Written authority for high-risk work", ["A licence to drive plant on public roads", "A pass to enter the site office", "A card proving your trade skills"],
 "A permit to work is a formal written system used for high-risk tasks such as hot work, confined spaces or live services. It sets out the precautions and who has authorised the work."),
("You see a hazard that is not part of your job. What should you do?",
 "Report it to your supervisor", ["Ignore it, it is not your area", "Fix it yourself whatever it is", "Mention it at the end of the week"],
 "Everyone on site has a duty to report hazards. Make the area safe only if you are competent to do so, and tell your supervisor straight away."),
("Your supervisor tells you to carry on with a task you believe is unsafe. What should you do?",
 "Stop and raise your concern", ["Carry on because you were told", "Finish the task as fast as possible", "Walk off site without telling anyone"],
 "You have the right to stop work you believe is unsafe. Explain your concern; if it is not resolved, take it higher, for example to the site manager or a safety representative."),
("Why must you sign in and out when you arrive at and leave site?",
 "To account for everyone in an emergency", ["So your hours can be checked for your pay", "So security can search your bag on exit", "So the client knows who is working today"],
 "Signing in and out gives a record of who is on site. In a fire or evacuation it is used to check that everyone has got out."),
("Who must provide you with information, instruction and training to work safely?",
 "Your employer", ["The local council", "The equipment supplier", "Your trade union"],
 "The 1974 Act and the Management Regulations place this duty on the employer. Suppliers must give product information, but training is the employer's job."),
("What should happen if the work method changes from what the risk assessment covers?",
 "The assessment is reviewed first", ["Work carries on as planned", "The old method is used instead", "Only the client is told about it"],
 "A risk assessment must be reviewed when the work, equipment or conditions change significantly. Stop and check with your supervisor before working in a different way."),
("Who is responsible for health and safety on a construction site?",
 "Everyone working on the site", ["Only the site manager and foreman", "Only the health and safety adviser", "Only the client"],
 "Employers, managers and workers all have legal duties. Every person on site must work safely and look out for others."),
("A worker under 18 joins your team. Why might extra controls be needed?",
 "They may lack experience of the risks", ["They are not allowed on any building site", "They need no site induction at all", "They may only work at weekends"],
 "Employers must take account of young persons' lack of experience and maturity in their risk assessment. Extra supervision and restrictions on some tasks are common."),
("What is the main role of a health and safety representative?",
 "To represent workers on safety matters", ["To enforce the law and issue fines", "To sign off every scaffold as safe to use", "To carry out all first aid on site"],
 "Safety representatives raise workers' concerns, are consulted by the employer and may inspect the workplace. Enforcement is the job of HSE inspectors."),
("Where would you normally find the site rules and emergency arrangements explained to you?",
 "At the site induction", ["On the delivery note", "In your contract of employment", "On the client's website"],
 "The site induction covers site rules, welfare, first aid, fire and emergency procedures. Ask if anything is unclear."),
("What does 'competent' mean when used for a person on site?",
 "Trained and experienced enough for the task", ["Has worked for the firm for over a year", "Holds a university degree in construction", "Is the oldest and longest-serving worker"],
 "A competent person has enough training, knowledge and experience to do the job safely and to recognise their own limits."),
("Who must make sure a construction phase plan is drawn up before the construction phase begins?",
 "The client", ["The site security", "Each worker", "The local planning office"],
 "Under CDM 2015 the client must ensure a construction phase plan is drawn up before the construction phase begins. The principal contractor, or the sole contractor on a smaller job, prepares it."),
("What is a hazard?",
 "Anything that could cause harm", ["The chance that harm will happen", "An accident that has happened", "A sign that warns you of danger"],
 "A hazard is something that can cause harm, such as a fragile roof or a noisy tool. Risk is the likelihood and severity of harm from that hazard."),
("What is meant by 'risk' in a risk assessment?",
 "How likely harm is and how bad it could be", ["Anything that could cause harm on site", "The cost of putting each hazard right", "A list of jobs that are not allowed on site"],
 "Risk combines the likelihood of harm with its severity. Controls aim to reduce risk to as low as reasonably practicable."),
("You have been briefed on a method statement but do not understand part of it. What should you do?",
 "Ask before starting the work", ["Guess and adjust as you go", "Follow what your mate does", "Skip that part of the task for now"],
 "You must understand the safe system of work before you start. Ask your supervisor to explain anything unclear."),
("Under CDM 2015, which of these has duties as a duty holder?",
 "Every worker on the project", ["Only the client and designer", "Only firms with over 5 staff", "Only those on notifiable jobs"],
 "CDM 2015 places duties on clients, designers, principal designers, principal contractors, contractors and workers. Workers must, for example, report anything likely to endanger themselves or others."),
("What should you do if you are injured at work, however minor?",
 "Report it so it is recorded", ["Treat it yourself and say nothing", "Wait to see if it gets worse", "Tell only your workmates about it"],
 "All injuries should be reported and recorded. The record helps the employer decide whether RIDDOR applies and supports any later claim."),
]
